WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:02.560
SPEAKER: Hidden in the
natural world around us

00:00:02.560 --> 00:00:06.010
are powerful biological tools
whose potential scientists are

00:00:06.010 --> 00:00:09.430
just beginning to unlock.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:12.100
For billions of years,
bacteria have been under attack

00:00:12.100 --> 00:00:13.630
from viruses.

00:00:13.630 --> 00:00:16.000
But bacteria developed
clever defense mechanisms

00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:19.260
to remember these
viruses and fight back.

00:00:19.260 --> 00:00:22.140
Recently, scientists harnessed
one of these natural tools

00:00:22.140 --> 00:00:26.010
called CRISPR Cas9 and created
a powerful gene editing

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.380
technology with the potential
to treat human disease.

00:00:29.380 --> 00:00:31.620
But Cas9 isn't the
only CRISPR tool

00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:33.960
with applications
for human health.

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:38.190
A team of scientists found that
a cousin of Cas9, called Cas13,

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:41.910
can be harnessed to actually
detect human disease.

00:00:41.910 --> 00:00:45.180
Here's how Cas13 works in
its natural environment.

00:00:45.180 --> 00:00:49.140
First it hunts for viral
RNA using an RNA guide.

00:00:49.140 --> 00:00:53.400
When it finds its viral target,
Cas13 becomes activated.

00:00:53.400 --> 00:00:55.890
In some circumstances,
it cuts any RNA

00:00:55.890 --> 00:00:59.700
it encounters-- a process
termed collateral cleavage.

00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:02.310
It is this mechanism that
scientists at the McGovern

00:01:02.310 --> 00:01:03.180
Institute.

00:01:03.180 --> 00:01:05.129
Broad Institute, and
Harvard University

00:01:05.129 --> 00:01:08.670
harnessed to create Sherlock,
a highly sensitive tool

00:01:08.670 --> 00:01:12.540
to detect infectious
diseases in humans.

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:15.580
Here's how the Sherlock
detection system works.

00:01:15.580 --> 00:01:17.610
First, researchers take
a sample from a patient

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:20.760
with a possible viral
infection, like the flu.

00:01:20.760 --> 00:01:23.370
Then they amplify the
levels of RNA in it

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:27.800
and add reporters that
are sensitive to Cas13.

00:01:27.800 --> 00:01:32.250
Then an engineered CRISPR
Cas13 is added to the sample.

00:01:32.250 --> 00:01:34.890
This Cas13 is
programmed with a guide

00:01:34.890 --> 00:01:40.230
RNA that is designed to find
only virus RNA and bind to it.

00:01:40.230 --> 00:01:43.170
When this happens, Cas13
activates its cleaving

00:01:43.170 --> 00:01:46.950
mechanism and begins
randomly slicing nearby RNA,

00:01:46.950 --> 00:01:49.020
including the reporters.

00:01:49.020 --> 00:01:51.840
Since each end of the reporter
carries a different label,

00:01:51.840 --> 00:01:54.150
Cas13 separates
these two signatures,

00:01:54.150 --> 00:01:57.640
creating a unique signal
within the sample.

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.090
The sample is then applied to
a commercial flow detection

00:02:00.090 --> 00:02:01.410
system.

00:02:01.410 --> 00:02:03.240
If the sample is
negative for flu,

00:02:03.240 --> 00:02:06.480
the reporter remains intact and
collects at the first detection

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:07.740
line.

00:02:07.740 --> 00:02:09.660
If the sample is
positive for flu,

00:02:09.660 --> 00:02:11.580
it collects at a
different location,

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.060
making a diagnosis easy to spot.

00:02:15.060 --> 00:02:17.730
So easy, in fact, Sherlock
can be used in the field

00:02:17.730 --> 00:02:20.880
to detect emerging infectious
diseases and outbreaks,

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:22.764
like Ebola virus.

00:02:22.764 --> 00:02:24.180
Before Sherlock,
field samples had

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.270
to be refrigerated and shipped
to labs with expensive gene

00:02:27.270 --> 00:02:29.520
sequencing equipment.

00:02:29.520 --> 00:02:33.240
Now Sherlock can be adapted to
detect any genetic signature,

00:02:33.240 --> 00:02:37.920
even ones associated with cancer
in virtually any location.

00:02:37.920 --> 00:02:41.070
CRISPR Cas9 and Cas13
are just two examples

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:43.740
of natural biological
systems that scientists

00:02:43.740 --> 00:02:47.820
have modified to fight genetic
and infectious disease.

00:02:47.820 --> 00:02:50.190
The question remains--
what other tools

00:02:50.190 --> 00:02:52.359
are out there in the
natural world just

00:02:52.359 --> 00:02:53.400
waiting to be discovered?

00:02:53.400 --> 00:02:55.850
[MUSIC PLAYING]

